<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Estado Novo (Portugal)</h1><p class="st-meta">1933 CE – 1974 CE · capital Lisbon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Salazar's authoritarian New State, one of Europe's longest-lived dictatorships, which fought to keep Portugal's African empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The constitution of 1933, approved by plebiscite, turned the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ditadura-nacional">Ditadura Nacional</a> into a corporatist regime under prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">António de Oliveira Salazar</a>, inspired by Catholic social teaching and the idea of an organic nation.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime banned parties except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-union-portugal">National Union</a>, censored the press and used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pide">PVDE</a>, later PIDE, secret police and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarrafal-camp">Tarrafal</a> prison camp against opponents. Portugal stayed neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, leased the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1949. Its economy remained poor, prompting mass emigration, while from 1961 it fought costly colonial wars in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcelo-caetano">Marcelo Caetano</a> succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">Salazar</a> in 1968 but failed to end the wars. On 25 April 1974 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armed-forces-movement">Armed Forces Movement</a> overthrew the regime in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-portuguese-republic">Third Portuguese Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/estado-novo-portugal.jpg" alt="Estado Novo (Portugal)" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Columbano Bordalo Pinheiro Vector: Vítor Luís Rodrigues, António Martins-Tuválkin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1933 CE – 1974 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lisbon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>António de Oliveira Salazar, Óscar Carmona, Marcelo Caetano</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Second Portuguese Republic, Salazarist Portugal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1954&amp;focus=estado-novo-portugal" aria-label="Estado Novo (Portugal) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4453 789 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Estado Novo (Portugal) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4773 1045L4773 1045M4773 1045L4773 1044M4773 1045L4773 1045"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1045h0M4773 1044h0M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lisbon. <a href="/atlas?year=1954&amp;focus=estado-novo-portugal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
